e] [17592186409920 1071 "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=(x+y+z)^{2}?$" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409920 1072 #uuid "00fd1a7d-44b2-529b-bcff-08a778520166" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409920 1074 "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ (x+y+z)^{2} & =c \\\\ x+y+z & =\\pm \\sqrt{c} \\\\ x+y+z\\pm \\sqrt{c} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)$ can't be negative. Therefore, we get a family of planes:\n\n$$\nx+y+z\\pm \\sqrt{c}=0,\\,c\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409919 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409921 1069 "q-71594" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409921 1071 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}$ that passes through the point $P(2,\\sqrt{5},3).$" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409921 1072 #uuid "595252cd-1afe-5f16-ae97-13c476966ff1" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409921 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,\\sqrt{5},3) & =\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2(2)^{2}+(\\sqrt{5})^{2}-(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=1.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(2,\\sqrt{5},3)$ is the surface ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=0.$" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409918 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409922 1069 "q-129189" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409922 1071 "Find the equation of the level surface for the function $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-z$ that passes through the point $P(0,-2,4).$" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409922 1072 #uuid "ef96fb6f-8651-5bcd-8700-e66e3e1a4bf5" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409922 1074 "First, we find the value of the function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,2,4) & ={2}^{2}-{0}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =4-0-4 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2}-{x}^{2}-z & =0 \\\\ z & ={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface that passes through the point $P(0,-2,4)$ is the hyperbolic paraboloid $z={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409923 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409927 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409923 1062 #uuid "a94aecad-6d06-5584-9ae6-ff5e4c972c6e" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409923 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409922 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409924 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409925 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409924 1062 #uuid "bfbdcc24-f373-559b-9261-b97d80ffba78" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409924 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409920 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409925 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409926 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409925 1062 #uuid "d981e48e-0b28-5d2c-b93e-70be17e1dbbd" 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409925 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409921 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409926 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409923 13194139533586 17592186045417 true] [17592186409926 1062 #uuid "f363fade-7d87-58eb-bf21-09d